1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,800
[blue Merc sings an A, 220 Hz]

2
00:00:03,150 --> 00:00:05,050
[orange Merc sings an A, 440 Hz]

3
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:06,457
ORANGE: Ha! Same note.

4
00:00:06,757 --> 00:00:07,590
BLUE: Is it?

5
00:00:07,723 --> 00:00:11,393
Mine shook the air 220 times a second.

6
00:00:11,643 --> 00:00:14,093
Yours shook it 440.

7
00:00:14,393 --> 00:00:17,160
ORANGE: Twice as fast?
Then why do they sound the same?

8
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:22,272
BLUE: Good question. To answer it,
we need to know what a note really is.

9
00:00:22,472 --> 00:00:25,672
[chime]

10
00:00:26,072 --> 00:00:29,666
Every note you have ever heard
starts with something shaking.

11
00:00:30,016 --> 00:00:31,367
A guitar string.

12
00:00:31,667 --> 00:00:32,734
A speaker.

13
00:00:33,034 --> 00:00:35,186
The vocal folds in your throat.

14
00:00:36,036 --> 00:00:40,229
Pluck a string, and it swings
back and forth, pushing on the air.

15
00:00:40,529 --> 00:00:42,631
Here it is, slowed right down.

16
00:00:43,031 --> 00:00:47,534
Each push travels outward as a wave,
all the way to your ear.

17
00:00:47,934 --> 00:00:52,292
Count the swings in one second,
and you have the note's frequency.

18
00:00:52,892 --> 00:00:57,378
At real speed,
this string swings 220 times.

19
00:00:57,678 --> 00:01:00,841
We measure it in hertz. Swings per second.

20
00:01:01,641 --> 00:01:03,710
Slow swings make a low note.

21
00:01:03,793 --> 00:01:05,941
[a tone sweeps up from 110 to 880 Hz]

22
00:01:06,241 --> 00:01:08,220
Fast swings make a high one.

23
00:01:08,820 --> 00:01:10,970
ORANGE: So a high note
is just a loud note?

24
00:01:11,218 --> 00:01:12,051
BLUE: No.

25
00:01:12,214 --> 00:01:14,216
Loud is how far it swings.

26
00:01:14,666 --> 00:01:16,102
High is how fast.

27
00:01:16,802 --> 00:01:20,107
MercuryPitch draws
your voice the same way.

28
00:01:20,191 --> 00:01:23,816
As you sing,
a line climbs when you go higher.

29
00:01:26,616 --> 00:01:28,210
Now, back to our puzzle.

30
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:30,559
Here is 220.

31
00:01:30,859 --> 00:01:33,132
And here is 440.

32
00:01:33,432 --> 00:01:35,357
Exactly twice as fast.

33
00:01:35,857 --> 00:01:40,296
Before you listen, predict.
Two different notes, or the same one?

34
00:01:40,596 --> 00:01:43,196
[pause and predict]

35
00:01:43,396 --> 00:01:44,796
[220 Hz]

36
00:01:45,096 --> 00:01:46,496
[440 Hz]

37
00:01:46,796 --> 00:01:48,796
[both together]

38
00:01:49,296 --> 00:01:53,216
Higher, yes. But together,
they melt into one note.

39
00:01:53,566 --> 00:01:55,138
That jump has a name.

40
00:01:55,488 --> 00:01:56,596
An octave.

41
00:01:57,396 --> 00:01:58,900
Now look at the waves.

42
00:01:58,983 --> 00:02:04,177
Every second peak of the fast one
lands right on a peak of the slow one.

43
00:02:04,477 --> 00:02:07,224
They line up again, and again, forever.

44
00:02:07,724 --> 00:02:11,695
ORANGE: Like us walking together.
I take two steps for every one of yours.

45
00:02:11,995 --> 00:02:17,108
BLUE: And we land together, every time.
Two for one: the simplest fit there is.

46
00:02:17,191 --> 00:02:18,472
That's the octave.

47
00:02:19,222 --> 00:02:26,395
Try 277 instead, and the peaks drift in
and out of step. That's a different note.

48
00:02:26,895 --> 00:02:30,036
Go down an octave, to 110.

49
00:02:30,286 --> 00:02:31,203
[A: 110 to 880 Hz]

50
00:02:31,286 --> 00:02:35,615
Then double, and double, and double.
The same letter, every time.

51
00:02:36,065 --> 00:02:41,108
The gaps in hertz keep doubling,
but every jump sounds the same size.

52
00:02:41,191 --> 00:02:44,089
Your ear hears multiplying, not adding.

53
00:02:46,989 --> 00:02:51,439
So pitch isn't really a straight line.
It's more like a spiral.

54
00:02:51,839 --> 00:02:56,548
Go up an octave, and you come back round,
right above where you started.

55
00:02:57,148 --> 00:03:01,078
Musicians name the notes on
each turn with seven letters.

56
00:03:01,278 --> 00:03:06,078
[eight notes: A B C D E F G A]

57
00:03:06,478 --> 00:03:07,754
ORANGE: Only seven notes?

58
00:03:08,054 --> 00:03:09,204
BLUE: Seven letters.

59
00:03:09,554 --> 00:03:10,636
Twelve notes.

60
00:03:10,986 --> 00:03:13,382
The other five are next time's story.

61
00:03:13,982 --> 00:03:18,605
A number tells you which turn you're on.
Each new turn starts at C.

62
00:03:19,005 --> 00:03:24,185
So the A that orchestras
tune to is A4. 440 Hz.

63
00:03:24,485 --> 00:03:29,434
Middle C on a piano is C4.
Same letters, different turns.

64
00:03:30,434 --> 00:03:33,629
Unroll the spiral, and you get the piano.

65
00:03:33,713 --> 00:03:37,231
The same seven letters,
octave after octave.

66
00:03:38,231 --> 00:03:43,589
When two people sing the same tune,
they're often an octave apart. Listen.

67
00:03:43,889 --> 00:03:48,489
[a tune, two voices an octave apart]

68
00:03:48,839 --> 00:03:50,533
Two voices, one tune.

69
00:03:50,833 --> 00:03:55,096
So when you match a note,
an octave up or down still counts.

70
00:03:55,596 --> 00:04:00,567
Let's try. I'll sing an A.
Hum it back, wherever it feels easy.

71
00:04:00,967 --> 00:04:03,167
[blue Merc sings an A]

72
00:04:03,367 --> 00:04:06,767
[your turn: hum an A]

73
00:04:06,967 --> 00:04:08,967
[orange Merc hums an A, an octave up]

74
00:04:09,217 --> 00:04:11,417
ORANGE: I hummed it higher.
Does that count?

75
00:04:11,560 --> 00:04:16,188
BLUE: Higher, but the same note.
That counts. Keep it gentle. Never push.

76
00:04:17,188 --> 00:04:18,134
Quick check.

77
00:04:18,434 --> 00:04:20,000
What makes a note higher?

78
00:04:20,300 --> 00:04:22,100
[think]

79
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:25,179
Faster swings. A higher frequency.

80
00:04:25,779 --> 00:04:28,119
Double the frequency, and you get?

81
00:04:28,419 --> 00:04:30,219
[think]

82
00:04:30,519 --> 00:04:32,369
ORANGE: The same note, one octave up!

83
00:04:32,943 --> 00:04:35,736
BLUE: And why do A3 and A4 share a letter?

84
00:04:36,036 --> 00:04:37,836
[think]

85
00:04:38,136 --> 00:04:43,531
Their waves fit two to one.
Same spot on the spiral, one turn higher.

86
00:04:44,531 --> 00:04:45,607
Next time.

87
00:04:45,907 --> 00:04:50,322
Seven letters, but twelve notes.
Where do the other five come from?

88
00:04:50,722 --> 00:04:51,822
ORANGE: See you there.

89
00:04:51,905 --> 00:04:56,757
[chime]
